<article class="env-theorem" id="024U">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/024U">Theorem <span data-tag="024U">41.6.2</span>.</a> Let $Y$ be a connected scheme. Let $f : X \to Y$ be unramified and separated. Every section of $f$ is an isomorphism onto a connected component. There exists a bijective correspondence </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \text{sections of }f \leftrightarrow \left\{  \begin{matrix}  \text{connected components }X'\text{ of }X\text{ such that}

\\  \text{the induced map }X' \to Y\text{ is an isomorphism} 

\end{matrix} \right\}   \]
</div>
  <p> In particular, given $x \in X$ there is at most one section passing through $x$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Direct from Proposition <a href="/tag/024T" data-tag="024T">41.6.1</a> part (3). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>